Space Complexity
Time & Space

What is time and space for an algorithm?
When you use code or an algorithm, it takes two important things: time (how fast it works) and space (how much memory it uses).

Time
An algorithm takes a certain amount of time to run, depending on its complexity and the size of the problem it's solving.
Space
Space complexity is how much computer memory an algorithm needs to complete a task.

Just like we care about an algorithm's time efficiency, we also focus on its space usage by aiming to minimize memory consumption, which is the essence of space complexity analysis.

Example 1: Addition Function
C++ Code

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int addition(int a, int b){
    int c = a + b;
    return c;
}
int main() {
    cout << addition(10,5); // Output - 15
    return 0;
}

@ The addition function uses a fixed amount of space regardless of the input values.
@ It needs space for three integers (a, b, and c) and a small amount of additional memory for the function call itself.
@ So, its space complexity will be O(3 + c(space for function call)) = O(1).

Now, let's consider optimizing the code to use less space. By eliminating the intermediate variable c and directly returning a + b, like this:

Optimized Code
C++ Code

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int addition(int a, int b){
    return a + b;
}
int main() {
    cout << addition(10,5); // Output - 15
    return 0;
}

@ In this case, it only requires memory for the input parameters 'a' and 'b', and a little bit of extra space for the function call itself.
@ Space complexity: O(2 + c (space for function call)) = O(1)

Example 2: Sum of Array Function
C++ Code

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int add_array(int arr[], int n){
     int sum = 0;
     for (int i = 0; i < n; i++){
        sum = sum + arr[i];
     }
     return sum;
}
int main() {
    int arr[] = {1,2,3,4,5};
    cout << add_array(arr,5);
    return 0;
}

@ the add-array function uses space depending upon the input value N (size of array)
@ it needs space for array, n, sum, i, and a small amount of additional memory for the function call itself.
@ So, its space complexity will be O( N(space for array) + 3(space for n, sum, i integers) + c(space for function call)) = O(N).

